1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,252
A kick makes a ball faster.

2
00:00:02,252 --> 00:00:04,237
A brake makes a bicycle slower.

3
00:00:04,237 --> 00:00:05,837
A force changes momentum.

4
00:00:06,237 --> 00:00:07,824
But what changes a spin?

5
00:00:08,274 --> 00:00:10,622
In the park, Aarav
pushes a merry-go-round.

6
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:12,678
A short push, and it turns slowly.

7
00:00:13,028 --> 00:00:16,082
He runs alongside and
keeps pushing for longer.

8
00:00:16,082 --> 00:00:17,867
Now it spins much faster.

9
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:19,949
Why does pushing longer matter?

10
00:00:20,449 --> 00:00:23,195
The answer links torque
to angular momentum.

11
00:00:23,795 --> 00:00:26,998
Remember, torque is the
turning effect of a force.

12
00:00:26,998 --> 00:00:30,938
Angular momentum, L, is how
much a body goes around a point.

13
00:00:31,288 --> 00:00:32,605
Here is the link.

14
00:00:32,605 --> 00:00:35,987
Torque is the rate at which
angular momentum changes.

15
00:00:36,387 --> 00:00:41,191
Just as a force changes momentum,
a torque changes angular momentum.

16
00:00:41,791 --> 00:00:44,024
Our motion notebook, from above.

17
00:00:44,024 --> 00:00:45,959
The merry-go-round is at rest.

18
00:00:45,959 --> 00:00:47,547
Its axle is the axis.

19
00:00:47,897 --> 00:00:52,004
Aarav pushes along the rim with fifty
newtons, one point five metres from

20
00:00:52,004 --> 00:00:52,904
the axle.

21
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:53,640
Watch.

22
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:56,582
The torque is fifty times one point five.

23
00:00:56,582 --> 00:00:58,498
Seventy five newton metres.

24
00:00:58,498 --> 00:01:01,175
L climbs by seventy five every second.

25
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:04,686
After two seconds, L is
one hundred and fifty.

26
00:01:04,686 --> 00:01:06,530
After four, three hundred.

27
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:08,539
Twice as long, twice the L.

28
00:01:09,039 --> 00:01:10,017
Slow-mo replay.

29
00:01:10,017 --> 00:01:11,516
Freeze during the push.

30
00:01:11,866 --> 00:01:14,268
r runs from the axle to his hands.

31
00:01:14,268 --> 00:01:15,751
F is along the rim.

32
00:01:15,751 --> 00:01:20,012
Their cross product, the torque,
points up, out of the screen.

33
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:22,058
And that is the way L grows.

34
00:01:22,458 --> 00:01:24,180
At four seconds, Aarav lets go.

35
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:25,569
Now you try.

36
00:01:25,569 --> 00:01:26,501
No friction.

37
00:01:26,501 --> 00:01:30,708
Once he lets go, will L drop,
stay the same, or keep growing?

38
00:01:30,708 --> 00:01:31,938
Pause and decide.

39
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:36,699
It stays at three hundred!

40
00:01:36,699 --> 00:01:38,693
No torque, so no change in L.

41
00:01:38,693 --> 00:01:42,006
The graph goes flat, and the
merry-go-round keeps turning.

42
00:01:42,606 --> 00:01:45,111
Let's prove the link, one step at a time.

43
00:01:45,461 --> 00:01:46,361
Step one.

44
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:49,109
For a particle, L equals r cross p.

45
00:01:49,859 --> 00:01:50,800
Step two.

46
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,508
Find how it changes.

47
00:01:52,508 --> 00:01:57,910
d L by d t equals d r by d t
cross p, plus r cross d p by d t.

48
00:01:58,660 --> 00:01:59,693
Step three.

49
00:01:59,693 --> 00:02:03,443
d r by d t is the
velocity v, and p is m v.

50
00:02:03,443 --> 00:02:07,160
They point the same way,
so v cross p is zero.

51
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:08,888
Step four.

52
00:02:08,888 --> 00:02:11,237
d p by d t is the force F.

53
00:02:11,237 --> 00:02:15,495
So d L by d t equals r cross
F, which is the torque.

54
00:02:16,245 --> 00:02:17,145
Step five.

55
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:21,372
For a body made of many particles,
the inside torques cancel in pairs.

56
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:23,861
Only outside torques change the total L.

57
00:02:24,611 --> 00:02:25,629
Remember this.

58
00:02:25,629 --> 00:02:28,713
Torque is the rate of
change of angular momentum.

59
00:02:29,413 --> 00:02:32,120
So why does a longer push spin it faster?

60
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:33,149
Link by link.

61
00:02:33,499 --> 00:02:36,186
His push makes a torque, r times F.

62
00:02:36,586 --> 00:02:39,021
Torque is the rate at which L grows.

63
00:02:39,021 --> 00:02:41,042
Every second adds seventy five.

64
00:02:41,442 --> 00:02:44,424
A longer push adds more
seconds, so more L.

65
00:02:44,424 --> 00:02:46,602
And more L means a faster spin.

66
00:02:47,202 --> 00:02:48,304
Side by side.

67
00:02:48,304 --> 00:02:51,448
In a straight line, a
force changes momentum.

68
00:02:51,448 --> 00:02:54,031
Force times time is the change in p.

69
00:02:54,431 --> 00:02:57,390
In turning, a torque
changes angular momentum.

70
00:02:57,390 --> 00:03:01,555
Torque times time is the change
in L, called the angular impulse.

71
00:03:02,155 --> 00:03:03,055
Your turn.

72
00:03:03,013 --> 00:03:06,512
One push gives a hundred
newton metres for one second.

73
00:03:06,512 --> 00:03:09,293
Another gives twenty
five for four seconds.

74
00:03:09,293 --> 00:03:10,663
Which adds more L?

75
00:03:10,663 --> 00:03:11,925
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:15,125 --> 00:03:16,241
Both the same!

77
00:03:16,241 --> 00:03:19,589
One hundred times one, and
twenty five times four.

78
00:03:19,589 --> 00:03:21,159
Each adds one hundred.

79
00:03:21,809 --> 00:03:23,499
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:23,849 --> 00:03:28,620
A zero point two kilogram ball goes
around on a zero point five metre string

81
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:30,428
at two metres per second.

82
00:03:30,428 --> 00:03:35,289
A push gives a torque of zero point
three newton metres, for two seconds.

83
00:03:35,289 --> 00:03:36,736
Find its new speed.

84
00:03:37,236 --> 00:03:39,011
First, L is m v r.

85
00:03:39,011 --> 00:03:42,473
Zero point two times two
times zero point five.

86
00:03:42,473 --> 00:03:43,774
Zero point two.

87
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:47,240
The change in L is torque times time.

88
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:49,241
Zero point three times two.

89
00:03:49,241 --> 00:03:50,483
Zero point six.

90
00:03:51,233 --> 00:03:53,267
So the new L is zero point eight.

91
00:03:54,017 --> 00:03:55,514
v is L over m r.

92
00:03:55,514 --> 00:03:58,190
Zero point eight over zero point one.

93
00:03:58,190 --> 00:03:59,985
Eight metres per second.

94
00:04:00,635 --> 00:04:02,096
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:02,446 --> 00:04:05,896
Many students think L stays the
same only when no force acts.

96
00:04:06,296 --> 00:04:07,196
Not true.

97
00:04:07,111 --> 00:04:10,022
The string pulls the
ball toward the centre.

98
00:04:10,022 --> 00:04:14,643
That pull is along r, so its torque
is zero, and L stays the same.

99
00:04:15,293 --> 00:04:16,829
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:16,829 --> 00:04:21,858
In torque as d L by d t, in angular
impulse, and in L of a falling body

101
00:04:21,858 --> 00:04:23,022
about a point.

102
00:04:23,622 --> 00:04:24,809
Exam level now.

103
00:04:24,809 --> 00:04:29,473
A half kilogram ball is dropped from
rest at the point two metres, twenty

104
00:04:29,473 --> 00:04:30,373
metres.

105
00:04:30,136 --> 00:04:31,333
Take g as ten.

106
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:35,203
Find its angular momentum about
the origin after one second.

107
00:04:40,603 --> 00:04:41,964
The answer is B.

108
00:04:41,964 --> 00:04:44,611
Ten kilogram metre squared per second.

109
00:04:45,111 --> 00:04:49,768
Gravity is five newtons, straight down,
and acts two metres from the origin.

110
00:04:49,768 --> 00:04:52,915
So the torque is ten newton
metres, all the time.

111
00:04:53,415 --> 00:04:54,852
L starts at zero.

112
00:04:54,852 --> 00:04:57,726
After one second, L is ten times one.

113
00:04:57,726 --> 00:04:58,626
Ten.

114
00:04:58,773 --> 00:05:01,855
Check: p is five, and
the moment arm is two.

115
00:05:01,855 --> 00:05:03,420
Five times two is ten.

116
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:05,561
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:07,145
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:10,345 --> 00:05:12,284
Can you answer these without help?

119
00:05:12,684 --> 00:05:13,584
One.

120
00:05:13,141 --> 00:05:16,091
Why does a longer push spin
a merry-go-round faster?

121
00:05:16,991 --> 00:05:17,891
Two.

122
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:19,300
Why is v cross p always zero?

123
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:21,100
Three.

124
00:05:20,684 --> 00:05:24,412
Why does the ball on a string keep
its L, though the string pulls it?

125
00:05:25,312 --> 00:05:28,242
Next time: a heavy and a
light friend on a see-saw.

126
00:05:28,242 --> 00:05:30,262
Where must each sit so it balances?
